{
  "level": "Year 8",
  "subject": "maths",
  "title": "Substituting integers into expressions",
  "frames": [
    {
      "heading": "Substituting negative numbers",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Substitute means replace a letter with a number.",
          "write": "x = −5"
        },
        {
          "say": "Real numbers are often negative. Temperatures, debts, depths below sea level."
        },
        {
          "say": "Golden rule. Put the number in brackets when you substitute.",
          "write": "4x → 4(−5)",
          "draw": {
            "type": "shapes",
            "width": 560,
            "height": 240,
            "alt": "4x becomes 4 brackets minus 5",
            "items": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "x": 140,
                "y": 110,
                "text": "4x",
                "size": 56,
                "color": "black",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "kind": "arrow",
                "x": 220,
                "y": 95,
                "x2": 300,
                "y2": 95,
                "color": "purple"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "x": 420,
                "y": 110,
                "text": "4(−5)",
                "size": 56,
                "color": "green",
                "change": "add",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "x": 280,
                "y": 200,
                "text": "4 × (−5) = −20",
                "size": 30,
                "color": "purple",
                "change": "add"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "Brackets keep the sign with the number and show the multiply.",
          "write": "4 × (−5) = −20"
        },
        {
          "say": "Writing 4 minus 5, or 45, would be wrong.",
          "write": "✗ 4−5   ✗ 45"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Substitute, then BIDMAS",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Evaluate 5 take 3x when x is minus 4.",
          "write": "5 − 3x,  x = −4"
        },
        {
          "say": "Substitute with brackets.",
          "write": "5 − 3(−4)",
          "diagram": "diagrams/y8/maths/substituting-integers-into-expressions-machine.svg",
          "alt": "x = −4 goes into times 3 to give −12, then 5 minus that gives 17"
        },
        {
          "say": "Multiply first. 3 times minus 4 is minus 12.",
          "write": "5 − (−12)"
        },
        {
          "say": "Two minus signs together make a plus.",
          "write": "5 + 12 = 17",
          "draw": {
            "type": "shapes",
            "width": 560,
            "height": 250,
            "alt": "Minus a negative becomes plus",
            "items": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "x": 280,
                "y": 100,
                "text": "5 − (−12)",
                "size": 48,
                "color": "black",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "kind": "rect",
                "x": 205,
                "y": 50,
                "w": 90,
                "h": 70,
                "fill": "none",
                "change": "highlight",
                "color": "red"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "x": 280,
                "y": 210,
                "text": "5 + 12 = 17",
                "size": 40,
                "color": "green",
                "change": "add",
                "bold": true
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "Don't drop the double sign. It is not 5 take 12.",
          "write": "✗ 5 − 12"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Negatives and powers",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Let x be minus 3. Then x squared is minus 3 times minus 3.",
          "write": "(−3)² = 9"
        },
        {
          "say": "Minus x squared means square first, then apply the minus.",
          "write": "−(−3)² = −9",
          "diagram": "diagrams/y8/maths/substituting-integers-into-expressions-powers.svg",
          "alt": "Square −3 to get 9, then take the opposite to get −9"
        },
        {
          "say": "In 2x squared, only x is squared. Not the 2.",
          "write": "2 × (−3)² = 18"
        },
        {
          "say": "With brackets, 2x squared means minus 6, squared.",
          "write": "(2x)² = (−6)² = 36",
          "draw": {
            "type": "shapes",
            "width": 560,
            "height": 290,
            "alt": "Four expressions with x = minus 3",
            "items": [
              {
                "kind": "rect",
                "x": 20,
                "y": 40,
                "w": 250,
                "h": 70,
                "fill": "blue",
                "label": "x² → 9"
              },
              {
                "kind": "rect",
                "x": 290,
                "y": 40,
                "w": 250,
                "h": 70,
                "fill": "red",
                "change": "highlight",
                "label": "−x² → −9"
              },
              {
                "kind": "rect",
                "x": 20,
                "y": 150,
                "w": 250,
                "h": 70,
                "fill": "blue",
                "change": "highlight",
                "label": "2x² → 18"
              },
              {
                "kind": "rect",
                "x": 290,
                "y": 150,
                "w": 250,
                "h": 70,
                "fill": "green",
                "label": "(2x)² → 36"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "x": 280,
                "y": 270,
                "text": "x = −3",
                "size": 26,
                "color": "black",
                "bold": true
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "Leaving out brackets is the big mistake. Minus 3 squared needs brackets.",
          "write": "✗ −3² = −9"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Minus x is not always negative",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Minus x means the opposite of x.",
          "write": "−x = opposite of x"
        },
        {
          "say": "If x is minus 3, then minus x is plus 3.",
          "write": "−(−3) = 3",
          "draw": {
            "type": "shapes",
            "width": 560,
            "height": 180,
            "alt": "Opposite of −3 is 3",
            "items": [
